    "slot_key": "Verification information needed to trust transformed results",
    "slot_value": "Users should be able to prepare inputs, run proven transformations, and inspect the results in the GUI; success also means the GUI accurately reflects the transformation results so users can trust what they see. Deeper inspection is especially needed when the program is unusual, the transformation is complex, the result is surprising, or a run fails; speed matters more for normal exploratory runs with consistent output. Users are trying to preserve the semantics of the original program and judge whether the transformed result is trustworthy for further analysis or reuse. If sharing, templates, search, audit trails, or collaboration are required for traceability or confidence in the transformed results, that specific piece should be pulled into scope for the first release. Anything that could break existing researcher workflows or change the meaning of results should go through coordination first, including major changes to the transformation invocation flow, saved application formats, or how outputs are displayed and interpreted. Submission issues should be caught up front with a helpful message before anything runs; transformation failures should be reported as system or service errors during execution; result problems should be shown as result-quality or validation issues after the transformation completes so researchers know whether to fix input, retry the service, or inspect the output more closely. The current run should keep its exact configuration as the record of what actually happened, while the reusable copy should be a separate editable object, versioned for evolution, and ideally linked back to the approved run or source run for traceability. For exported results, researchers should be able to save the transformed program in a machine-readable form and, when useful, in a shareable review view; the export format should depend on the state being exported, since an intermediate review view may need different output than a finalized transformed result. Exports should also preserve enough provenance to show which transformation and inputs produced them, including the source program version or input file, the exact transformation name and version, the run timestamp, key options or constraints used, and any proof or validity check with whether the output was fully within proven scope or came from a warning path. Additional provenance for exports is partly undecided: versioning and validation status are important, source traceability should be included in principle so exports can get back to the originating input and run, and the exact fields should be marked to be confirmed until underlying service capabilities are verified. A direct link back to the specific source transformation run or state is preferred for exported outputs; if that link is not possible, provenance should at minimum include a unique run identifier. The export should carry the run identifier, parent or source run reference, recovery or retry markers, timestamps, the transformation version or service version, and a clear record of which outputs were original versus recovered or rerun. It should also include enough context to tie the export back to the exact transformation configuration and any checkpoints used, so someone can audit it later without guessing. I’d only omit fields that are purely local UI details, like temporary layout state, but anything that affects traceability or reproducibility should stay with the export. Existing primary runs and their derived retry records should remain immutable once recorded, so their provenance doesn’t shift after the fact. If the transformation system changes in a way that alters how lineage is interpreted, the UI and backend should version that interpretation rather than rewrite historical records, so older exports and audits still point to the meaning that was valid at the time. For comparisons, the system should preserve backward compatibility as much as possible and, if needed, show a clear notice that older records were produced under a previous lineage scheme.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

